WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:13.090
URS HOLZLE: Thank you.

00:00:13.090 --> 00:00:17.050
Way too much credit, Diane,
but welcome to Horizon.

00:00:17.050 --> 00:00:19.490
And as Diane
mentioned, we've been

00:00:19.490 --> 00:00:24.000
building a hyperscale cloud and
running it for quite a while.

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:29.920
And it has over
eight applications

00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:33.380
powered by this cloud with
more than a billion users--

00:00:33.380 --> 00:00:36.250
Seach, Maps, YouTube,
and so on and so on.

00:00:36.250 --> 00:00:39.150
And as Diane mentioned,
the eighth one

00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:43.220
is actually TCP Google
Cloud Platform itself.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:47.000
So every day, Google Cloud
Platform applications--

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:49.540
not by Google, by
our customers-- touch

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:52.990
over a billion
different IP addresses

00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:55.480
worldwide every single day.

00:00:55.480 --> 00:00:57.160
So it's really an
incredible scale.

00:00:57.160 --> 00:01:00.790
And that scale requires
an incredible investment.

00:01:00.790 --> 00:01:06.880
I'm very proud that the 10-plus
years of investment-- not just

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:11.730
money but really expertise,
very hard work by a very

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.890
large team-- is being
recognized today.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:16.760
And I want to start
off by just showing you

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:22.850
how we're different from
other clouds in a few ways.

00:01:22.850 --> 00:01:26.240
So the first one Diane
already mentioned a little bit

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:28.080
is our network.

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:33.480
It's probably one of our
biggest areas of investment.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:39.580
We have hundreds of thousands
of miles of fiber that we own.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:42.980
The largest, fastest, highest
capacity submarine cable

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:47.020
in the world, for example,
was built by Google.

00:01:47.020 --> 00:01:49.550
And generally
speaking, you can reach

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:54.540
with this private backbone
pretty much any place on Earth.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:57.820
That really means
pretty much any place.

00:01:57.820 --> 00:02:01.230
So, for example, we have
physical infrastructure

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:03.200
in Bhutan.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.520
If you don't know where
it is, look it up.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.160
Really wonderful country.

00:02:08.160 --> 00:02:14.700
Next, we also have operated
at scale for a very long time.

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:17.130
And if you operate
at our scale, you

00:02:17.130 --> 00:02:21.170
realize that efficiency and
performance is important.

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:22.760
And so we had lots
of time and lots

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:29.020
of expertise to really
do that and it shows.

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:32.650
Another TCP first
is VM migration.

00:02:32.650 --> 00:02:34.800
Just because we do
maintenance or just

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.790
because we have
to do an upgrade,

00:02:36.790 --> 00:02:38.950
you shouldn't need
to do anything.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:43.060
So on TCP, despite us
upgrading things all the time,

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.810
your workload just
keeps running.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:51.710
And of course, we provide
great value-- thank you,

00:02:51.710 --> 00:02:58.460
Lydia-- on up to 59% less
for the same workload

00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.100
at great performance.

00:03:00.100 --> 00:03:04.750
And what's more important, we
put the cloud back in cloud

00:03:04.750 --> 00:03:08.660
because with per minute
billing, custom VM

00:03:08.660 --> 00:03:12.260
types, automatic
discounts, no worrying

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:15.110
about reserved instances
or long-term contracts,

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:17.070
you pay as you go.

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.740
That's what cloud
was invented for.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:24.260
And so with our
approach to pricing--

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.660
our user-friendly
approach to pricing--

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.410
we're just much easier
to do business with.

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:37.070
So of course, we're
improving this product.

00:03:37.070 --> 00:03:38.970
Diane talked a little
bit about the launches.

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:41.290
I want to highlight
just a few things that

00:03:41.290 --> 00:03:44.320
launched in the past few weeks.

00:03:44.320 --> 00:03:48.010
One was our SQL service V2.

00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:52.560
It's the highest throughput
SQL service on any cloud.

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.420
Automated storage
sizing for SQL.

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:58.600
So you never run out of storage
space for your database,

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:01.710
because we grow it
automatically for you.

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:04.410
Weather data for BigQuery
if you want to correlate

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.240
your data with weather.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:09.850
New machine, learning APIs.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:13.740
VM rightsizing recommendations
to save you money.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:16.009
Lower pricing for
preemptable VMs.

00:04:19.329 --> 00:04:21.709
Another first-- resizing
persistent disks

00:04:21.709 --> 00:04:24.700
without downtime while
your VM keeps running.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:27.860
So it's just a few
of the improvements

00:04:27.860 --> 00:04:30.920
that we launched in just
the last four to six weeks.

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:34.820
Now, we see incredible demand
from pretty much anywhere

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:35.720
in the world.

00:04:35.720 --> 00:04:41.740
And to make sure that all users
have a cloud next to them,

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:45.400
we're going to roll out new
regions at a rapid pace.

00:04:45.400 --> 00:04:53.940
In the UK, in
Germany, in Finland,

00:04:53.940 --> 00:05:05.650
and in India, and Singapore,
and Australia, and Brazil.

00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:07.760
And the last country,
northern Virginia.

00:05:10.730 --> 00:05:14.070
And so we're actually
going to-- and this

00:05:14.070 --> 00:05:16.540
is on top of Oregon, which
we rolled out earlier.

00:05:16.540 --> 00:05:19.820
Japan is coming next
week and next month.

00:05:19.820 --> 00:05:22.640
And starting in
2017, we're going

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.200
to roll out roughly one
new region per month,

00:05:27.200 --> 00:05:29.570
and we're going to keep up
that pace for quite a while.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:31.900
So this is just the first
wave of our expansion.

00:05:31.900 --> 00:05:36.210
You're going to hear more about
the next wave in the coming

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:37.030
months.

00:05:37.030 --> 00:05:40.340
All of these regions are
going to be multi-zone,

00:05:40.340 --> 00:05:43.690
so you can run high
availability applications

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:45.580
in a single region.

00:05:45.580 --> 00:05:48.280
And they're going to be
connected by the Google Cloud

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.900
Network-- the largest private
backbone in the world.

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:55.300
So your connectivity is going
to be fast no matter where

00:05:55.300 --> 00:05:58.580
you are and no matter where your
users are because you can reach

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:03.830
them using the same connectivity
that Google reaches that user.

00:06:03.830 --> 00:06:07.650
All right, now, I
told you that we're

00:06:07.650 --> 00:06:08.802
building a different cloud.

00:06:08.802 --> 00:06:10.510
And I want to tell
you a little bit more,

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:13.860
but to do that, let
me go a little bit

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.960
about the history of clouds.

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:19.320
So cloud in many ways
started about 20 years ago

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:21.250
with colocation.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.900
Colocation let you rent
data center space instead

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:27.560
of having to worry
about investing

00:06:27.560 --> 00:06:31.110
building, maintaining,
running a data center.

00:06:31.110 --> 00:06:32.350
So that was great.

00:06:32.350 --> 00:06:35.250
And pretty soon
afterwards, it went really

00:06:35.250 --> 00:06:38.020
to the second wave
of cloud, where

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:40.720
all of this infrastructure
was virtualized.

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.050
So you had the same components--
servers, disks, networks,

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:47.420
whatever, load balancers--
but now they're

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:49.970
virtual, not physical.

00:06:49.970 --> 00:06:54.420
And that's great because what
used to be a purchase order now

00:06:54.420 --> 00:06:56.900
becomes an API
call and, poof, you

00:06:56.900 --> 00:06:59.810
have a new machine or a
new disk or a new whatever.

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:03.670
And that really transformed
how people do IT.

00:07:03.670 --> 00:07:05.930
It's a very, very deep impact.

00:07:05.930 --> 00:07:10.520
But it maintained the same
structure of the data center.

00:07:10.520 --> 00:07:12.050
Everything is
virtual now, but you

00:07:12.050 --> 00:07:15.000
deal with the same components,
and it's just as hard

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:17.710
to put them together
and to maintain them

00:07:17.710 --> 00:07:20.140
and to actually
keep things running.

00:07:20.140 --> 00:07:22.520
So that forces the
user to still focus

00:07:22.520 --> 00:07:25.290
a lot of their effort
on the wrong things.

00:07:25.290 --> 00:07:26.794
Do I have enough servers?

00:07:26.794 --> 00:07:27.960
Do they have the right type?

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.350
Did I purchase too much
or maybe too little?

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:32.910
Do I need to upgrade my OS?

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:34.510
Do I have a security alert?

00:07:34.510 --> 00:07:37.800
All of these things
your staff is

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:42.020
forced to pay attention
to instead of writing

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:45.520
new applications,
creating value, having

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:47.530
innovative applications.

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:50.170
And innovative
applications is ultimately

00:07:50.170 --> 00:07:52.620
how businesses win,
how they get new users,

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.230
how they get new
markets, how they

00:07:54.230 --> 00:07:56.730
make their employees happy.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:59.940
So until about 10 years
ago, we were actually

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:01.090
in that same boat.

00:08:01.090 --> 00:08:03.240
Our internal infrastructure
looked about like that,

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.220
and we were struggling
with all of that.

00:08:05.220 --> 00:08:08.450
And we realized that we
can't really maintain

00:08:08.450 --> 00:08:09.550
our pace of innovation.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:13.870
We can't really be Google
if we don't get out

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:15.050
of this complexity.

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:17.070
So about 10 years
ago, we really started

00:08:17.070 --> 00:08:18.460
to redo our infrastructure.

00:08:18.460 --> 00:08:21.200
We started with a
container-based model,

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.070
moving away from machines,
and with automated services

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:26.900
and scalable data.

00:08:26.900 --> 00:08:32.380
And that lets our developers
enjoy unprecedented scale,

00:08:32.380 --> 00:08:35.490
reliability, security
without worrying

00:08:35.490 --> 00:08:38.570
that much about the
infrastructure behind it.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:42.390
And this is what we have
brought to Google Cloud Platform

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:44.490
and that many of you
benefit from today.

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:46.230
Let me just give
you a few examples.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:49.430
For example, App Engine--
Google App Engine--

00:08:49.430 --> 00:08:51.470
lets you write an app.

00:08:51.470 --> 00:08:53.180
You never have to
worry about servers.

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:55.280
You never have to worry
about operating systems.

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:57.950
You don't even have to
worry about regions,

00:08:57.950 --> 00:09:00.430
zone outages, nothing.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:02.790
You can have an
ambitious application,

00:09:02.790 --> 00:09:05.790
you can run it for a year,
and you can have literally--

00:09:05.790 --> 00:09:10.640
and I mean literally-- zero
hours on administration.

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.490
Or take another
example, BigQuery,

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:17.050
our analytic solution, there is
no provisioning of an analytics

00:09:17.050 --> 00:09:17.572
cluster.

00:09:17.572 --> 00:09:18.530
There's no maintenance.

00:09:18.530 --> 00:09:19.400
There's no sizing.

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:20.760
There's no upgrading.

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:23.980
Just bring your data
and start querying.

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:28.660
Or Container Engine runs
your Kubernetes containers

00:09:28.660 --> 00:09:31.650
without you having to
really administer anything.

00:09:31.650 --> 00:09:34.710
Or even for conventional
things like Hadoop clusters,

00:09:34.710 --> 00:09:37.160
you can be up and
running in 90 seconds,

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:41.160
have your Hadoop cluster,
zero administration.

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:45.230
So no ops is really a big step.

00:09:45.230 --> 00:09:48.050
And the third wave
of computing I think

00:09:48.050 --> 00:09:52.160
needs to focus on making all of
the power of cloud accessible

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:57.160
without you worrying about
the details behind it.

00:09:57.160 --> 00:10:01.540
Now, Google was founded
with an ambitious mission--

00:10:01.540 --> 00:10:03.720
organize all the
world's information.

00:10:03.720 --> 00:10:06.570
And that obviously required
us to become experts

00:10:06.570 --> 00:10:09.820
at handling lots of data.

00:10:09.820 --> 00:10:11.210
Just for Search.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:14.480
For Search, we have
to store and index

00:10:14.480 --> 00:10:18.500
and serve a private
copy of the entire web.

00:10:18.500 --> 00:10:21.160
And we've been doing
that for 15 years.

00:10:21.160 --> 00:10:24.810
We've had our first
trillion-line log analysis

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:27.860
running sometime
in the early 2000s.

00:10:27.860 --> 00:10:31.210
So we've been dealing with
big data for a long time,

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:34.580
and that forced us
to really pioneer

00:10:34.580 --> 00:10:38.650
many of the tools and
techniques that are used today

00:10:38.650 --> 00:10:40.730
in big data.

00:10:40.730 --> 00:10:43.030
And we're a
data-driven business.

00:10:43.030 --> 00:10:45.580
And so this wasn't just
about infrastructure,

00:10:45.580 --> 00:10:47.420
this was about
creating tools that

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:52.470
lets many Google employees
analyze and understand

00:10:52.470 --> 00:10:54.620
the data that we have.

00:10:54.620 --> 00:10:57.410
So let me talk a little
bit more about that,

00:10:57.410 --> 00:11:00.260
because externally in
Google Cloud Platform,

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:04.300
we took these internal tools
and made them available to you.

00:11:04.300 --> 00:11:07.620
And they were really making a
big impact to our customers.

00:11:07.620 --> 00:11:09.660
The key tool is BigQuery.

00:11:09.660 --> 00:11:11.870
BigQuery is our
analytic solution.

00:11:11.870 --> 00:11:15.520
And it lets you take pretty much
an unlimited amount of data--

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:19.640
and I mean that literally--
and run SQL queries against it

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:22.850
and get results in seconds.

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:26.980
Don't have to worry about
scalability or maintenance

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:30.690
or anything-- no provisioning,
no maintenance, zero ops.

00:11:30.690 --> 00:11:32.930
So it's really been a
game changer for many.

00:11:32.930 --> 00:11:35.790
And I'm very excited
today to announce

00:11:35.790 --> 00:11:38.750
that we have a major
new version of BigQuery

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:41.576
available-- BigQuery
for Enterprise.

00:11:41.576 --> 00:11:45.520
One of the key changes in
BigQuery for Enterprise

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:49.340
is that you can
now update tables.

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.670
So that turns it from
an analytic solution

00:11:51.670 --> 00:11:54.680
into a full data warehouse.

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:58.940
So BigQuery Enterprise comes
with many very nice features.

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:00.705
I can mention just a few.

00:12:00.705 --> 00:12:03.100
I already mentioned
support for DML.

00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:06.910
So you can do updates,
deletes on your data.

00:12:06.910 --> 00:12:10.620
It supports SQL, so
all of your tools

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:12.980
or pretty much all of
your tools will work out

00:12:12.980 --> 00:12:16.840
of the box with
BigQuery Enterprise.

00:12:16.840 --> 00:12:19.355
Heavy users can
get flat pricing,

00:12:19.355 --> 00:12:21.690
a predictable bill every month.

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.800
I'll talk more about
that afterwards.

00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:27.280
And of course, like
all of our services,

00:12:27.280 --> 00:12:29.650
you have identity
and access management

00:12:29.650 --> 00:12:33.560
that gives you security and
compliance for your data

00:12:33.560 --> 00:12:36.164
and access to your data.

00:12:36.164 --> 00:12:38.830
We have lots of other features I
can't talk about-- for example,

00:12:38.830 --> 00:12:40.530
just like in Google
Docs, we tried

00:12:40.530 --> 00:12:41.850
to make collaboration easy.

00:12:41.850 --> 00:12:46.630
So sharing queries is as
easy as sharing a link.

00:12:46.630 --> 00:12:50.760
And of course, being from
Google, it's extremely fast.

00:12:50.760 --> 00:12:52.770
We asked a third party
to run some benchmarks

00:12:52.770 --> 00:12:57.240
based on the industry
standard TCPH benchmarks.

00:12:57.240 --> 00:13:02.671
Here's a graph that shows
when multiple users run

00:13:02.671 --> 00:13:08.870
TCPH query number
10-- no, number 22

00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:10.870
on 10 terabytes of data.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:12.650
Sorry.

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:15.080
So when you have a few
users, both systems

00:13:15.080 --> 00:13:16.360
are reasonably fast.

00:13:16.360 --> 00:13:20.620
But as you increased
usage, of the power

00:13:20.620 --> 00:13:24.910
and the automatic scalability of
BigQuery really starts to show.

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:29.050
And at 32 users,
it's 29 times faster

00:13:29.050 --> 00:13:31.960
because BigQuery can scale
automatically for you.

00:13:31.960 --> 00:13:35.020
And it scales independently
of the data size.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:37.660
So you get just the
right performance

00:13:37.660 --> 00:13:39.590
at a very attractive price.

00:13:39.590 --> 00:13:41.390
Let me actually
elaborate on that.

00:13:41.390 --> 00:13:42.930
So it is high
performance does not

00:13:42.930 --> 00:13:48.100
come at the penalty of
paying through your nose.

00:13:48.100 --> 00:13:51.430
It's not just easier to use,
it's also cheaper to use.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.330
Now how is that?

00:13:53.330 --> 00:13:55.510
Here's another comparison.

00:13:55.510 --> 00:13:57.670
This assumes that
you have a data

00:13:57.670 --> 00:14:00.170
warehouse that grows over time.

00:14:00.170 --> 00:14:02.790
And so you get not just
the higher performance

00:14:02.790 --> 00:14:06.430
that you just saw, but you
get low predictable cost.

00:14:06.430 --> 00:14:11.250
Because BigQuery only uses
resources when you are actually

00:14:11.250 --> 00:14:12.330
running queries.

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:13.900
So let me give you an analogy.

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:16.610
It's a little bit
like a light switch.

00:14:16.610 --> 00:14:21.220
So with Amazon Redshift,
you have to pick a room size

00:14:21.220 --> 00:14:25.354
and you have to pick the number
of light bulbs in that room.

00:14:25.354 --> 00:14:27.020
And then you have to
turn it, on and you

00:14:27.020 --> 00:14:30.220
have to leave it turned
on for the entire month

00:14:30.220 --> 00:14:32.520
whether you're in
the room or not.

00:14:32.520 --> 00:14:34.680
And no surprise, your bill
at the end of the month

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:36.096
is going to be
pretty substantial.

00:14:36.096 --> 00:14:38.970
In comparison, BigQuery
is a little bit more

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:42.310
like a motion
sensor light switch.

00:14:42.310 --> 00:14:45.120
So the light goes on when
you enter the room, goes off

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:46.190
when you leave it.

00:14:46.190 --> 00:14:48.520
Magically, the room is
just always well-lit

00:14:48.520 --> 00:14:49.630
and the right size.

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:52.240
And so no wonder,
your bill can be lower

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:54.400
at the end of the month.

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:58.740
So you get this scalability
and the zero administration

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:03.060
at a much lower price
than comparable solutions.

00:15:03.060 --> 00:15:05.320
So with that, it's
really pretty easy

00:15:05.320 --> 00:15:08.370
to choose your cloud data
warehouse because really

00:15:08.370 --> 00:15:10.480
no other data
warehouse offers you

00:15:10.480 --> 00:15:16.010
a combination of
these functionalities

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:19.320
and the choice
between pay as you go,

00:15:19.320 --> 00:15:22.750
which is ideal for light
users, and fixed pricing, which

00:15:22.750 --> 00:15:25.860
is ideal for heavy users.

00:15:25.860 --> 00:15:30.110
So that much about analytics--
and obviously, analytics

00:15:30.110 --> 00:15:31.860
is really, really
important, because it

00:15:31.860 --> 00:15:35.790
lets you take your data
and derive value from it.

00:15:35.790 --> 00:15:39.100
But often, machine
learning can actually

00:15:39.100 --> 00:15:42.980
derive even bigger value
from that same data.

00:15:42.980 --> 00:15:45.140
To give an example,
just a few months ago,

00:15:45.140 --> 00:15:48.810
we have applied machine
learning to our own data centers

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:52.120
and to the controls of the
cooling system of that data

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:52.660
center.

00:15:52.660 --> 00:15:57.770
We were able to save up to 40%
of cooling energy in our data

00:15:57.770 --> 00:15:59.580
centers by applying
machine learning.

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:01.150
So it's really terrific.

00:16:01.150 --> 00:16:03.720
The problem is that
machine learning

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.430
has a high hurdle to adoption.

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:09.710
You need a lot of expertise
and a lot of compute

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:11.870
to actually make
it work for you.

00:16:11.870 --> 00:16:14.240
And our goal is to change that.

00:16:14.240 --> 00:16:17.140
Our goal is to democratize
machine learning

00:16:17.140 --> 00:16:20.250
and really make it possible
for everyone to use it.

00:16:20.250 --> 00:16:24.880
And how we do that I'll show
you in the next few minutes.

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:28.260
The key to it is cloud
machine learning,

00:16:28.260 --> 00:16:30.790
and easiest way to use
cloud machine learning

00:16:30.790 --> 00:16:33.440
is to use complete solutions.

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:37.830
So we launched a strong set
of machine learning APIs that

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:40.230
solve common problems for you.

00:16:40.230 --> 00:16:42.180
For example, here's
a sound file.

00:16:42.180 --> 00:16:47.330
Tell me what's spoken
in that sound file.

00:16:47.330 --> 00:16:49.610
These are trained
machine learning

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:52.460
models-- trained by
us, maintained by us,

00:16:52.460 --> 00:16:54.580
fully come as a service.

00:16:54.580 --> 00:16:58.380
Basically, bring your
data and get knowledge,

00:16:58.380 --> 00:17:00.170
insight out of it.

00:17:00.170 --> 00:17:03.080
These have been launched
throughout the year

00:17:03.080 --> 00:17:06.980
and have actually been some
of our most popular APIs ever.

00:17:06.980 --> 00:17:09.589
So usage is skyrocketing.

00:17:09.589 --> 00:17:12.349
But on top of having these
canned solutions that

00:17:12.349 --> 00:17:14.710
solve common problems,
you also need

00:17:14.710 --> 00:17:17.260
to be able to build
your own solution.

00:17:17.260 --> 00:17:20.410
And for that, we created
Cloud Machine Learning.

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:23.390
It went alpha early this
year, and today, I'm

00:17:23.390 --> 00:17:26.839
excited to announce that it's
now available to everyone

00:17:26.839 --> 00:17:30.026
as Cloud Machine Learning beta.

00:17:30.026 --> 00:17:33.040
It comes with a number
of great features.

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:36.010
First, should be
no surprise by now

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:39.550
that it is a no ops
machine learning solution.

00:17:39.550 --> 00:17:42.210
So you don't have
to create a training

00:17:42.210 --> 00:17:46.470
cluster or a prediction
cluster or choose machine size

00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:48.230
or whatever.

00:17:48.230 --> 00:17:50.050
You just focus on your problem.

00:17:50.050 --> 00:17:52.180
And once you have your
machine learning model,

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:55.990
you just install it and
we autoscale it for you

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:57.440
to arbitrary demand.

00:17:57.440 --> 00:18:02.830
So zero ops to run
and train your models.

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:04.910
Of course, you get
performance as well.

00:18:04.910 --> 00:18:06.290
So you can do training at scale.

00:18:06.290 --> 00:18:09.360
You get the full power--
the parallelized computing--

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:10.770
of the Google Cloud.

00:18:10.770 --> 00:18:14.760
And so model size is not
going to be a problem.

00:18:18.030 --> 00:18:19.960
It comes with a nice UI as well.

00:18:19.960 --> 00:18:21.890
You're going to
see that in a demo.

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:24.580
Data Lab is kind of
like a notebook that

00:18:24.580 --> 00:18:28.820
lets you develop your
code but also document it

00:18:28.820 --> 00:18:30.590
and share it very easily.

00:18:30.590 --> 00:18:34.550
And you can also manage
your training runs,

00:18:34.550 --> 00:18:36.330
your different versions.

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:39.630
You can connect to BigQuery
or to Google Cloud Storage.

00:18:39.630 --> 00:18:43.600
So it's easy to build a
full solution, not just

00:18:43.600 --> 00:18:45.170
a machine learning model.

00:18:45.170 --> 00:18:48.300
And last but not least, we
have a very special feature

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:50.130
that we call Hypertune.

00:18:50.130 --> 00:18:51.840
It's a little bit
esoteric, but when

00:18:51.840 --> 00:18:53.650
you build a machine
learning model,

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:55.390
one of the most
important things is

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:57.250
to get the model roughly right.

00:18:57.250 --> 00:19:00.090
But then, usually
today, that would

00:19:00.090 --> 00:19:03.520
be followed by days
and weeks of tuning,

00:19:03.520 --> 00:19:05.430
because this model has
a lot of parameters

00:19:05.430 --> 00:19:09.250
and you need to find out which
settings actually maximize

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:11.400
the accuracy of your model.

00:19:11.400 --> 00:19:16.760
And Google Cloud ML Hypertune
does that maximization for you.

00:19:16.760 --> 00:19:21.380
So we try lots of versions
in the background,

00:19:21.380 --> 00:19:23.750
and you can accomplish
in an hour what

00:19:23.750 --> 00:19:27.575
today takes an expert days
or even weeks to get to.

00:19:27.575 --> 00:19:28.950
And you'll see
that a little bit.

00:19:28.950 --> 00:19:31.570
It's really an
incredible timesaver.

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:34.890
But rather than me
talking to slides,

00:19:34.890 --> 00:19:37.050
let's actually see
that in action.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:40.860
I'd like to invite Rob
Craft up to the stage,

00:19:40.860 --> 00:19:45.292
and he's going to give you a
demo of a few different ways

00:19:45.292 --> 00:19:46.750
how customers use
machine learning.

00:19:46.750 --> 00:19:49.840
Rob, over to you.

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:51.006
ROB CRAFT: Thanks very much.

00:19:51.006 --> 00:19:51.958
Good morning.

00:19:56.480 --> 00:19:57.370
So is my mic on?

00:19:57.370 --> 00:19:59.680
It is on, awesome.

00:19:59.680 --> 00:20:05.680
So what you're looking at is
80,000 Creative Common images

00:20:05.680 --> 00:20:09.530
that we put through
our Vision API service.

00:20:09.530 --> 00:20:11.630
As I mentioned, this is
a fully trained model

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:13.290
based on a lot of
learnings we had

00:20:13.290 --> 00:20:15.400
creating Google
Photos, which does

00:20:15.400 --> 00:20:18.250
an amazing job about
understanding cognitively

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:20.750
what you are showing
it in pixel form.

00:20:20.750 --> 00:20:23.150
So here, we mapped 80,000
worth, and I plugged it

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:24.944
into a nice pretty word cloud.

00:20:24.944 --> 00:20:26.860
That gives you an idea
of how these things map

00:20:26.860 --> 00:20:28.400
that make sense to people.

00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:31.390
And then we have a couple
images to the side that shows

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:33.240
you a little bit of the power.

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:34.710
So the first is
the recognition--

00:20:34.710 --> 00:20:36.710
and forgive me for the
analogy, but the internet

00:20:36.710 --> 00:20:38.930
is powered with cats.

00:20:38.930 --> 00:20:41.930
And here is a cat to prove it.

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:44.970
Urs has private islands
bought with cat money

00:20:44.970 --> 00:20:46.210
I think at this point.

00:20:46.210 --> 00:20:48.430
So there's two things
that are important here.

00:20:48.430 --> 00:20:50.570
One is that we're
successfully labeling

00:20:50.570 --> 00:20:52.340
on the side what the image is.

00:20:52.340 --> 00:20:54.950
And as you might suppose,
close up, far away,

00:20:54.950 --> 00:20:57.086
color of, long hair, short
hair, looking at camera,

00:20:57.086 --> 00:20:59.710
not looking at camera-- you have
to have a pretty sophisticated

00:20:59.710 --> 00:21:01.850
understanding of
visualization systems

00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:03.570
to get this sort
of thing correct.

00:21:03.570 --> 00:21:06.410
But you want to know how
correct it is because it

00:21:06.410 --> 00:21:08.100
could be a business decision.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:10.584
You never believe anyone
without data, of course,

00:21:10.584 --> 00:21:12.250
if you're running a
successful business.

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:13.820
So on the side here, we're
giving you an indication

00:21:13.820 --> 00:21:15.250
of how certain we are.

00:21:15.250 --> 00:21:17.470
So we're 99% sure it's a cat.

00:21:17.470 --> 00:21:20.344
And it's 83% sure it's
a domestic short hair.

00:21:20.344 --> 00:21:22.510
We give you lots of other
information like the color

00:21:22.510 --> 00:21:23.120
palette.

00:21:23.120 --> 00:21:25.620
For those you with brand
sensitivity for colors

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:28.170
and textures and fields for
your advertising campaigns,

00:21:28.170 --> 00:21:30.155
this matters a lot and
it's really hard to get.

00:21:30.155 --> 00:21:31.530
And we offer a
lot of other data,

00:21:31.530 --> 00:21:33.830
like is it
inappropriate content?

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:36.390
90% of the new data
arriving in your business

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:38.110
is unstructured data like this.

00:21:38.110 --> 00:21:40.190
It's voice, it's audio,
it's in a language

00:21:40.190 --> 00:21:41.310
that you don't speak.

00:21:41.310 --> 00:21:42.660
It's directly from a customer.

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:44.201
It's about your
brand on the internet

00:21:44.201 --> 00:21:45.610
that you can't find yourself.

00:21:45.610 --> 00:21:47.360
Systems like that
it's drastically

00:21:47.360 --> 00:21:50.690
change the capability to
understand how people are

00:21:50.690 --> 00:21:53.200
talking about you, how you
would like to be talking,

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:54.830
how to be talked about.

00:21:54.830 --> 00:21:56.860
And as well as what
imagery matters.

00:21:56.860 --> 00:21:59.870
We also do it for the other
languages and capabilities.

00:21:59.870 --> 00:22:02.440
So let me do one more and I'll
flip over to another demo.

00:22:02.440 --> 00:22:06.180
This one is pretty exciting for
those of us that like sports.

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:08.490
On the surface, for
those of us from the US,

00:22:08.490 --> 00:22:10.910
this is very obviously
a baseball stadium.

00:22:10.910 --> 00:22:12.590
A computer can't
make that assumption,

00:22:12.590 --> 00:22:13.850
so we start from scratch.

00:22:13.850 --> 00:22:15.470
We don't believe
you when you label

00:22:15.470 --> 00:22:18.011
this is a baseball stadium, for
example, because it turns out

00:22:18.011 --> 00:22:20.680
people aren't always necessarily
truthful on the internet.

00:22:20.680 --> 00:22:22.770
I know it's surprising,
but there we are.

00:22:22.770 --> 00:22:24.220
So it's a stadium.

00:22:24.220 --> 00:22:25.254
It's a team sport.

00:22:25.254 --> 00:22:27.170
We're even picking up
some baseball equipment.

00:22:27.170 --> 00:22:28.400
You see the little picture
in the background--

00:22:28.400 --> 00:22:30.274
the guy holding a baseball
bat-- we picked up

00:22:30.274 --> 00:22:32.157
that it's a baseball
bat, not just a stick.

00:22:32.157 --> 00:22:34.240
And here is the part that
I'm especially proud of,

00:22:34.240 --> 00:22:36.560
not only do we grab the
text on all the signage

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:39.200
in the background-- and we're
offering bounding boxes soon

00:22:39.200 --> 00:22:40.658
so you can pick
what area the image

00:22:40.658 --> 00:22:42.650
you want to look at--
but we identified out

00:22:42.650 --> 00:22:46.010
of all the baseball
stadiums, this is Citi Field.

00:22:46.010 --> 00:22:47.804
So that capability
applied to landmarks

00:22:47.804 --> 00:22:49.220
using the power
of what we learned

00:22:49.220 --> 00:22:51.710
from Maps and other systems
becomes available for you.

00:22:51.710 --> 00:22:55.060
And to do one of these images
is literally six lines of code.

00:22:55.060 --> 00:22:57.210
So this vastly changes
the world around

00:22:57.210 --> 00:22:58.804
what machine
learning can provide

00:22:58.804 --> 00:23:00.220
versus the work
you think you have

00:23:00.220 --> 00:23:03.160
to do to get machine learning
benefits in your applications.

00:23:03.160 --> 00:23:04.850
So we're done.

00:23:04.850 --> 00:23:07.447
Everyone ready to do their
first neural network?

00:23:07.447 --> 00:23:08.530
Let's do a neural network.

00:23:08.530 --> 00:23:09.775
This will be fun.

00:23:09.775 --> 00:23:11.660
So we're going to start
with something that

00:23:11.660 --> 00:23:12.940
matters a lot for this room.

00:23:12.940 --> 00:23:14.670
Let's predict
whether the S&amp;P 500

00:23:14.670 --> 00:23:16.510
is going to close up or down.

00:23:16.510 --> 00:23:18.440
In this case,
we're going to take

00:23:18.440 --> 00:23:22.250
50 years plus worth of data
across 3,200 stock tickers.

00:23:22.250 --> 00:23:25.630
We're going to combine it
using some training information

00:23:25.630 --> 00:23:28.872
across other pieces, pump
it through a neural network,

00:23:28.872 --> 00:23:31.080
and out the other side is
going to come a prediction.

00:23:31.080 --> 00:23:34.000
This identifies what a lot
of people in your businesses

00:23:34.000 --> 00:23:36.700
are doing today or that you'll
be asked to do going forward,

00:23:36.700 --> 00:23:38.700
which is create your own model.

00:23:38.700 --> 00:23:40.604
So we're using the
Google Data Lab tool

00:23:40.604 --> 00:23:42.270
that Urs mentioned a
little bit earlier.

00:23:42.270 --> 00:23:44.620
It's a notebook
that has compile.

00:23:44.620 --> 00:23:47.390
You can actually compile queries
and do visualizations natively

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:48.470
in the here.

00:23:48.470 --> 00:23:51.440
An example of a visualization
of a data set is here.

00:23:51.440 --> 00:23:53.250
You can see that it's
a little bit noisy,

00:23:53.250 --> 00:23:55.640
so we're going to precondition
some data to clean it up.

00:23:55.640 --> 00:23:57.290
Much tighter
distribution of data

00:23:57.290 --> 00:24:00.440
sets, so we're going to
learn faster with our model.

00:24:00.440 --> 00:24:02.040
And what does a model look like?

00:24:02.040 --> 00:24:04.750
Well, in this case--
pardon the lingo drop,

00:24:04.750 --> 00:24:06.770
but I need to show
that it's a real thing.

00:24:06.770 --> 00:24:09.250
It's a feed four neural
network, four hidden layers,

00:24:09.250 --> 00:24:11.059
1,024 nodes per layer.

00:24:11.059 --> 00:24:13.100
We're doing rectified
linear [? units ?] between,

00:24:13.100 --> 00:24:14.770
and then we have a
soft max regression.

00:24:14.770 --> 00:24:17.070
A long way of saying this
is a reasonable demo.

00:24:17.070 --> 00:24:19.240
It ain't production, so
please don't do trading

00:24:19.240 --> 00:24:21.940
based on the numbers you're
going to see at the bottom.

00:24:21.940 --> 00:24:23.890
I said it-- I am
not responsible.

00:24:23.890 --> 00:24:26.950
So this is a prettier
picture for those of us

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:28.900
that forget everything
that I just said.

00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:31.370
It's a reasonable explanation
of what we're trying to do.

00:24:31.370 --> 00:24:33.390
This is how terse the code is.

00:24:33.390 --> 00:24:36.800
The tooling and machine
learning is really, really hard.

00:24:36.800 --> 00:24:37.820
We're doing it better.

00:24:37.820 --> 00:24:39.330
We're making it more simple.

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:42.070
The fact that it isn't scribbled
on a napkin or a whiteboard

00:24:42.070 --> 00:24:43.800
and erased as soon as
the data scientists

00:24:43.800 --> 00:24:46.800
leave the room is a big step
forward for the marketplace,

00:24:46.800 --> 00:24:49.460
and we're going to continue
to improve the experience.

00:24:49.460 --> 00:24:52.610
Let me get to the money
and the power here.

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:55.810
Use Cloud ML because it
takes a long time to train.

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:57.910
Let me skip right
to the money shot.

00:24:57.910 --> 00:25:01.330
It's 123 hours to do
what I just explained

00:25:01.330 --> 00:25:03.840
on roughly a developer
workstation class machine.

00:25:03.840 --> 00:25:06.130
Come back in five days
and you get your answer.

00:25:06.130 --> 00:25:07.880
Come back in five days
and get your answer

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:09.920
and then begin iteration.

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:12.400
Wait another five days or
argue about it for three days

00:25:12.400 --> 00:25:14.090
and then come back
in another five days.

00:25:14.090 --> 00:25:16.030
Welcome to the machine
scientist's nightmare.

00:25:16.030 --> 00:25:17.630
That's the state
of the art today.

00:25:17.630 --> 00:25:20.210
By just changing
one line, you say,

00:25:20.210 --> 00:25:22.336
I want to run it on
premium instance.

00:25:22.336 --> 00:25:24.210
I don't configure anymore
machines, come back

00:25:24.210 --> 00:25:25.640
in three hours.

00:25:25.640 --> 00:25:28.360
And if you want to go more,
we can go more if you'd like.

00:25:28.360 --> 00:25:30.570
Don't contact me
directly, we have people.

00:25:30.570 --> 00:25:33.760
So now you enter into
the interesting space

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:35.430
of it's OK but not great.

00:25:35.430 --> 00:25:36.810
The accuracy isn't what I want.

00:25:36.810 --> 00:25:39.060
It's 60%, and I'm not
going to make a business

00:25:39.060 --> 00:25:40.670
decision on 60%.

00:25:40.670 --> 00:25:43.700
If only there were a wand
with some magic pixie dust in.

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:45.330
Well, it's not quite
magic pixie dust.

00:25:45.330 --> 00:25:47.610
It's a lot of PhD sweat and
tears that gone into it,

00:25:47.610 --> 00:25:50.630
but Hypertune allows you
to automatically tune

00:25:50.630 --> 00:25:52.520
the aspects for the
model that may not

00:25:52.520 --> 00:25:55.150
be learning or asymptotic
towards the result

00:25:55.150 --> 00:25:57.490
that you're asking for the
model to predict against.

00:25:57.490 --> 00:26:00.080
So through one simple
change of code--

00:26:00.080 --> 00:26:02.160
it literally is
one line of code,

00:26:02.160 --> 00:26:04.510
and if Rob can find it
quickly-- There we go.

00:26:04.510 --> 00:26:07.040
Jobs trainer task.

00:26:07.040 --> 00:26:09.200
Off you go, and
you get the machine

00:26:09.200 --> 00:26:11.910
now spinning up multiple
multiple instances,

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:14.000
running multiple
experiments at once,

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:16.750
picking which one is maximizing
towards the prediction

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:18.830
that you want, and
rendering an answer.

00:26:18.830 --> 00:26:20.750
So there is what you
need to do if you're

00:26:20.750 --> 00:26:22.249
